[[1468,577,"illicit drugs, [REDACTED] were readily available within HMP Woodhill","illicit drug availability",""],[1469,577,"Failure consistently to identify, record and respond to prisoners under the influence","inconsistent identification of intoxicated prisoners",""],[1470,577,"relevant risk information was spread across multiple recording systems and was not always shared effectively between operational staff and clinical teams","fragmented information sharing",""],[1471,577,"blocked observation panels were not consistently challenged or cleared, and that visual welfare checks were therefore not always effective","blocked observation panels",""],[1472,577,"systems for identifying and managing prisoners who remain behind their door because of debt, fear, vulnerability or drug-related pressures were not sufficiently robust, coordinated or escalated","inadequate self-isolation management",""],[1473,577,"Mr Meikle was not subject to ACCT proceedings, despite evidence shortly before death of self-isolation, debt-related vulnerability, known substance misuse and reduced engagement. This","absence of ACCT despite vulnerability indicators",""],[1474,577,"prisoners serving IPP sentences may experience hopelessness, chronic frustration, deterioration in mental health and increased vulnerability to substance misuse and self-neglect","IPP sentence vulnerability",""],[1475,577,"prisoners with known vulnerabilities, substance misuse history and symptoms of deteriorating mental health may not always receive timely psychiatric assessment or sufficiently proactive mental health review","delayed psychiatric input",""],[1476,577,"staff responding to collapse were adequately trained and equipped to consider synthetic cannabinoid intoxication promptly as a possible cause","inadequate emergency response training",""],[1477,577,"staffing pressures, weak supervision, poor staff-prisoner engagement, restricted regimes, and inadequate welfare observations","staffing and regime limitations",""],[1478,577,"concerns about drugs, safety, violence, self-isolation, observation panel compliance, ACCT weaknesses and welfare monitoring at HMP Woodhill had been identified over time","repeated systemic concerns",""],[1479,577,"material information at the eleventh hour","delayed information provision",""],[2693,1032,"Local Procedures must ensure that staff understand they should not delay summoning emergency assistance","delayed emergency assistance",""],[2694,1032,"It is essential that an ambulance is called in all cases where there are serious concerns about the health of a prisoner and that access to both the prison and the individual prisoner is not delayed","failure to call ambulance",""],[2695,1032,"A representative NHS Ambulance guide for use in the community states that an ambulance should be called when there are signs of chest pain, difficulty in breathing, unconsciousness, severe blood loss, severe burns or scalds, choking, fitting or concussion, severe allergic reactions or a suspected stroke","inadequate emergency response criteria",""],[2696,1032,"Paragraph 5.7 indicates a number of minimum requirements for local protocols, including to inform staff that if they are in any doubt about the nature of the injury, they must call an ambulance","lack of clear staff guidance",""],[2697,1032,"The evidence of OSG [REDACTED] did not appear to demonstrate familiarity with the processes set out in this policy at the time of George's death","insufficient staff training on emergency protocols",""],[2698,1032,"The circumstances anticipated by this policy include situations where a prisoner's death may be prevented with appropriate application of an emergency response","preventable death due to protocol failure",""]]